A string is beautiful exactly when every aligned pair `(s[0], s[1])`, `(s[2], s[3])`, and so on has two equal characters.

**Why this holds:**
- If every aligned pair is equal, each pair is itself a valid substring of even length with one repeated character. So the string can be partitioned into those pairs.
- Conversely, take any valid partition. Every substring has even length, so each substring starts at an even index and covers whole aligned pairs. Each substring is all `0`s or all `1`s, so both characters in every pair inside it are equal.

**Counting changes:** Pairs are independent of each other. A pair whose two characters differ needs exactly one change, and a pair that already matches needs none. The answer is the number of aligned pairs with differing characters.

