If any element is smaller than k, it is impossible, because operations can only lower values. Otherwise, the answer is the number of distinct values strictly greater than k.

Why this count is the minimum:
- Each operation can only change elements that all share the current maximum value, and it lowers them to one new value.
- So each operation removes at most one distinct value from the set of values above k.
- We can always achieve exactly one operation per distinct value: pick h equal to the next smaller distinct value (or k at the end). This is valid because everything above h is the current maximum.

This matches Example 3, where the values above k = 1 are 9, 7, 5, 3, giving 4 operations.

```python
