The array can be fully split exactly when it is short or when two adjacent elements are large enough together.

**Small cases.** If n ≤ 2, the answer is always true. An array of length 1 needs no splits. An array of length 2 splits into two single elements, and length-one pieces are always allowed.

**Larger arrays (n ≥ 3).** The answer is true if and only if some pair of adjacent elements has sum ≥ m.

- **Necessity.** Look at the step just before the last pieces become singletons. At some point we must split an array of length 3 or more, since we start from n ≥ 3. One of its two resulting parts has length at least 2, so that part's sum must be ≥ m. Repeating this argument, at some point an array of length 2 must exist with sum ≥ m. That length-2 array is a pair of adjacent elements.
- **Sufficiency.** Suppose the pair at positions i and i+1 has sum ≥ m. Peel off one end element at a time, keeping the pair inside the remaining block. Each remaining block contains the pair, and all elements are positive, so its sum is ≥ m. Each peeled-off piece is a single element, which is always valid. Finally, split the pair itself into two single elements.

