Group the indices by value. For one value with sorted positions p, take a window of its occurrences from index l to index r. Making those occurrences contiguous means deleting every other element between them. That count is (p[r] - p[l] + 1) - (r - l + 1) = (p[r] - r) - (p[l] - l).

So the window is valid when this count is at most k. Use a sliding window over each value's positions: extend r, and move l forward while the deletion count exceeds k. The answer is the largest window size r - l + 1 seen across all values. Each position is visited a constant number of times, so the whole thing runs in O(n).

```python
from typing import List
from collections import defaultdict

class Solution:
    def longestEqualSubarray(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:
        pos = defaultdict(list)
        for i, x in enumerate(nums):
            pos[x].append(i)
        best = 0
        for p in pos.values():
            l = 0
            for r in range(len(p)):
                while (p[r] - r) - (p[l] - l) > k:
                    l += 1
                if r - l + 1 > best:
                    best = r - l + 1
        return best
```

